      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sula got its name in 1889 when settlers honored Ursula Thompson, the first non‑Indian child born in Ross’s Hole.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sula%2C_Montana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The valley that became Sula was originally called Ross’s Hole after fur trader Alexander Ross, who passed through in 1824.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sula%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lewis and Clark’s 1805 expedition was the first recorded American presence in the area, noting the first white man on the Bitterroot River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128209",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Wood “Longhaired” Thompson arrived in 1887 and bought out ranchers who had quit after the devastating 1886‑87 winter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/128209",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Sula sits along the East Fork of the Bitterroot River at the west end of Ross’ Hole, a wide valley surrounded by mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sula%2C_Montana",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Just 13 miles from the Idaho border, Sula offers downhill and cross‑country skiing in winter and hiking, camping, and fishing in summer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
